    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> regard the Guru Granth Sahib as the final, sovereign and eternal Guru, following the ten human gurus, and treat it with the honours due to a living person.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-arjan">Guru Arjan</a> compiled its first rendition in 1604, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-gobind-singh">Guru Gobind Singh</a> added the hymns of Guru Tegh Bahadur to produce the final edition.</li><li>Its hymns are arranged mainly by the rāga in which they are sung, and they include verses by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> Bhakti sants and a Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> saint as well as by the gurus.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Copies of the Guru Granth Sahib that are no longer fit to be read from are cremated, in a ceremony similar to the one for a deceased person, called Agan Bheta.</p></aside>
    </section>

